 audience but  rather live on Facebook and the Berlin  Philharmonic played Berio and Bartok to  an empty house music lovers were able to  livestream the concert into their living  rooms  [Music]  how much do we really need to be  together Thomas we saw in that in the  first sequence from Italy people out  there on their balconies and not only  there our balconies playing a big role  in Italy in Spain people are apparently  doing Fitness on their balconies with a  trainer on a rooftop instructing them do  you think things like that that we saw  things like what we saw in the report  are just temporary distractions or do  they actually help to build that sense  of solidarity that you were talking  about I absolutely think that they help  to build that sense of solidarity I  think you know in times of crisis around  the world you can have the worst out of  people but you can also get the best out  of people and these examples that we're  just seeing here are how people are  being very creative to try and make sure  that is very difficult a very bad  situation for many people can be an at  least a little bit improved in many  different ways this is something that  you also see in other parts of the world  by the way this is also you're also  starting to see right here in Germany I  saw something very interesting in recent  days of people asking to buy vouchers of  their favorite restaurants that they can  then redeem later on when the crisis has  been overcome I thought there was a  fantastic idea try and help your  favorite restaurant or your favorite  supermarket or a favorite shop they can  obviously not sell things now as they  would before but that's a way in which  you can help so I definitely think it's  high time for solidarity great thank you  Barbara in your opening statement you  said that the cessation of sporting  events would deprive many people of a  very important social support that they  need in a time like this so is there any  substitute for that in 